data: {"id":"chatcmpl_7eb52154-2b84-4164-a8f1-d9df92072e4d","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792098288,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"Referee assessmentC1 is well posed and matches an existing conjecture. I classify it as open in the inspected literature—not as a new conjecture, a proved theorem, or a refuted statement. The exact earlier formulation is Conjecture 3.1, equation (37), in On the Efficiency of Random Permutation for ADMM and Coordinate Descent [opened: arXiv:1503.06387]. arXivNo PDF attachment was available in this conversation. This report therefore concerns the complete inline statement and supplied diagnostic excerpts; it does not certify any unseen manuscript.1. Definitions and quantifiersThe intended assertion is\n$$\\begin{gathered}\nn,N\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1},\\qquad\nP_i\\in\\mathbb R^{N\\times N},\\qquad\nP_i^{\\mathsf T}=P_i,\\quad P_i^2=P_i,\\\\\nM=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nP_i,\\qquad\nS=\\frac1{n!}\\sum_{\\sigma\\in\\mathfrak S_n}\nP_{\\sigma(1)}\\cdots P_{\\sigma(n)},\\\\\nM^n-S\\succeq0.\n\\end{gathered}$$\nHere  $\\succeq0$  means\n$$x^{\\mathsf T}(M^n-S)x\\ge0\n\\quad\\text{for every }x\\in\\mathbb R^N.$$\nThe matrices are orthogonal projections. There is no assumption of mutual commutativity, distinctness, fixed rank, or any relation between  $n$  and  $N$ . In particular, repeated matrices and the zero and identity projections are allowed. The  $n!$  summands count permutations of labels, even when some matrices coincide.Although individual products need not be symmetric, their full permutation average is symmetric: transposition reverses the order, and reversal permutes the summands. Thus the positive-semidefinite comparison is meaningful. The only editorial clarification needed is to declare the dimension domain explicitly. Allowing  $N=0$  under a zero-dimensional matrix convention adds only a vacuous case;  $n=0$  is excluded because the mean is undefined.2. Prior formulation and established baselineThe identification with the earlier conjecture requires only expanding  $M,S$  and reversing the permutation order. It does not require adding hypotheses from the surrounding coordinate-descent application. That source separately establishes the weaker inequality\n$$S\\preceq M$$\nas Lemma 3, equation (52). arXiv+1This distinction remains visible in the later Provable Benefit of Random Permutations over Uniform Sampling in Stochastic Coordinate Descent [opened: arXiv:2505.23152]: Appendix E.2 explicitly contrasts the weak bound with the expression containing the  $n$ -th power. The inspected discussion does not supply a general resolution of C1. arXivThe elementary and commutative boundaries should also be acknowledged. The  $n=1$  case is equality by definition. The two-matrix case is covered by the established matrix-order inequality in §3.1 of Toward a Noncommutative Arithmetic-geometric Mean Inequality: Conjectures, Case-studies, and Consequences [opened: https://proceedings.mlr.press/v23/recht12/recht12.pdf]. Its Appendix A supplies the simultaneous-diagonalization and scalar-mean baseline for mutually commuting positive-semidefinite matrices. These cover restrictions of C1, not its unrestricted noncommuting domain. Proceedings of Machine Learning Research+1The remaining obligation is precise. Projection hypotheses give  $0\\preceq M\\preceq I$ , hence  $M^n\\preceq M$ . Consequently, the established bound  $S\\preceq M$  cannot simply be strengthened by transitivity to  $S\\preceq M^n$ : the proposed right-hand side is smaller. This identifies where binding to the available baseline stops; it is not a proposed proof.3. Why nearby counterexamples do not settle C1Two distinctions are essential.Recht–Ré Noncommutative Arithmetic-Geometric Mean Conjecture is False [opened: arXiv:2006.01510] concerns general positive-semidefinite matrices and norm-based comparisons, including an equivalent normalized scalar-identity bound. Its negative result cannot be transferred to C1 merely by observing that projections are positive semidefinite: a counterexample in a larger class need not belong to the projection subclass. Its three-factor result likewise bounds the symmetrized product between scalar multiples of the identity, rather than by the actual matrix  $M^3$ . arXiv+1Random Reshuffling is Not Always Better [opened: https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2020/file/42299f06ee419aa5d9d07798b56779e2-Paper.pdf] does include a projection-based counterexample in §2.3. However, that example refutes a comparison of averages of individual product norms. It is not presented there as a refutation of the matrix-order assertion C1. Moving a norm inside or outside an average changes the assertion. NeurIPS ProceedingsMore generally, a norm estimate gives a scalar bound on spectral magnitude; C1 asks for a directional quadratic-form bound using  $M^n$ . Conversely, a one-sided matrix-order bound alone does not control negative eigenvalues sufficiently to establish a two-sided norm assertion.I located no matching proof or refutation of C1 in this bounded search. The open classification rests on the exact prior conjecture and the inspected subsequent literature, not on treating an unsuccessful search as evidence of novelty.4. Correctness, escape, information gain, and utilityCorrectness: not_checked. No proof is submitted, and this report does not attempt one or construct a counterexample.Conditional proof escape: content, relative to the inspected established baseline. An affirmative resolution on the full stated domain would add the missing universal matrix-order bound; the accepted weak bound and known restrictions do not supply it by instantiation or normalization. This is a conditional assessment, not certification of an unseen proof. The statement itself is already present in prior literature. Proof shape is therefore null, and there are no live proof witnesses.Utility: the prospective result is a general theorem, with no need for an artificial downstream application. Nevertheless, this submission establishes no new theorem, refutation, checker, or certified instance. Established result-level utility is therefore insufficient, while the literature supports the mathematical relevance of the question.Information gain and settlement: no quantitative information model is supplied, so information gain is null. No settlement is claimed in the confirmed metadata, so settlement is null.The six supplied diagnostic excerpts do not change these findings. They concern, respectively, invariant states of positive trace-preserving maps, integrality of graph mixing expressions, scalar mean identities, inclusions between matrix cones, projection-valuation constraints, and orthogonality counts for qutrit measurement effects. None states the required universal permutation-product inequality. Their supplied declarations and metadata were compared as text, not independently kernel-checked or validated against a canonical publication.Recommendation: revise the presentation to attribute the existing conjecture, state its quantifiers explicitly, and distinguish the exact order assertion from related norm problems. This is editorial feedback, not a publication decision.JSON{\n  \"recommendation\": \"revise\",\n  \"summary\": \"C1 is a well-posed universal matrix-order assertion for real orthogonal projections. It is exactly an existing conjecture, rather than a newly posed question. Its status is classified as open within the inspected literature. The established weak bound, known restricted cases, and nearby norm-based results do not settle the full assertion. No proof is submitted or checked.\",\n  \"strengths\": [\n    \"The assertion has a precise interpretation after explicitly defining the two averages and the dimension domain.\",\n    \"An exact prior formulation was located and opened, allowing a substantive literature comparison rather than a novelty inference from diagnostic no-matches.\",\n    \"The difference between the established weak bound and the proposed matrix power identifies a concrete conditional mathematical obligation.\"\n  ],\n  \"concerns\": [\n    \"The statement needs attribution to the existing projection-matrix conjecture.\",\n    \"Related positive-semidefinite matrix and norm-based conjectures have different hypotheses or conclusions; their results must not be substituted for C1.\",\n    \"No attachment was available, and the inline sub"},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl_7eb52154-2b84-4164-a8f1-d9df92072e4d","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792098288,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"mission contains no proof or refutation.\",\n    \"The bounded literature search does not certify the absence of every later resolution.\"\n  ],\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"C1\",\n      \"correctness\": \"not_checked\",\n      \"proof_shape\": null,\n      \"witnesses\": [],\n      \"note\": \"Well-posedness and literature status are assessed separately from proof correctness. The exact question is already in the literature. Conditional escape is content relative to the inspected established baseline, but no actual proof shape or witness is certified. General-theorem utility is prospective rather than established by this statement-only submission. No quantitative information gain or named-problem settlement is claimed.\",\n      \"conjecture\": {\n        \"well_posed\": true,\n        \"well_posed_reason\": \"Interpret n and N as positive integers, P_i as real N-by-N matrices satisfying P_i^T=P_i and P_i^2=P_i, M=(1/n) sum_i P_i, and S=(1/n!) sum over all permutations sigma of P_sigma(1)...P_sigma(n). The conclusion is x^T(M^n-S)x\u003e=0 for every real vector x. Reversal of permutations makes S symmetric. Repeated matrices, arbitrary ranks, and zero or identity projections are allowed. No commutativity or relationship between n and N is assumed. An optional N=0 convention only adds a vacuous case.\",\n        \"status\": \"open\",\n        \"status_reason\": \"C1 matches Conjecture 3.1, equation (37), in the opened arXiv version of the Sun-Luo-Ye paper. The inspected subsequent sources do not provide a matching full-domain proof or refutation. The 2025 coordinate-descent discussion again distinguishes the weak bound from the expression with the n-th power. This is a bounded literature classification, not an exhaustive certification of current worldwide status.\",\n        \"named_works\": [\n          \"On the Efficiency of Random Permutation for ADMM and Coordinate Descent [opened: arXiv:1503.06387]\",\n          \"Toward a Noncommutative Arithmetic-geometric Mean Inequality: Conjectures, Case-studies, and Consequences [opened: https://proceedings.mlr.press/v23/recht12/recht12.pdf]\",\n          \"Recht–Ré Noncommutative Arithmetic-Geometric Mean Conjecture is False [opened: arXiv:2006.01510]\",\n          \"Random Reshuffling is Not Always Better [opened: https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2020/file/42299f06ee419aa5d9d07798b56779e2-Paper.pdf]\",\n          \"Provable Benefit of Random Permutations over Uniform Sampling in Stochastic Coordinate Descent [opened: arXiv:2505.23152]\"\n        ],\n        \"known\": [\n          \"On the Efficiency of Random Permutation for ADMM and Coordinate Descent [opened: arXiv:1503.06387]\",\n          \"Toward a Noncommutative Arithmetic-geometric Mean Inequality: Conjectures, Case-studies, and Consequences [opened: https://proceedings.mlr.press/v23/recht12/recht12.pdf]\",\n          \"Recht–Ré Noncommutative Arithmetic-Geometric Mean Conjecture is False [opened: arXiv:2006.01510]\",\n          \"Random Reshuffling is Not Always Better [opened: https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2020/file/42299f06ee419aa5d9d07798b56779e2-Paper.pdf]\",\n          \"Provable Benefit of Random Permutations over Uniform Sampling in Stochastic Coordinate Descent [opened: arXiv:2505.23152]\"\n        ],\n        \"escape\": \"content\",\n        \"escape_reason\": \"Conditionally, establishing the full noncommuting matrix-order bound would exceed the established weak inequality and the known restricted cases by more than renaming, instantiation, or normalization. This assessment is relative to the inspected baseline and does not certify a nonexistent submitted proof or absolute novelty. Merely restating the already published conjecture supplies no new theorem.\",\n        \"suggestions\": [\n          \"Write the formulas for M and S explicitly, specify positive integer dimensions, and say that permutations are of labels even when matrices repeat.\",\n          \"Attribute the exact prior conjecture and identify the established weak inequality separately; do not present C1 as an original question.\",\n          \"State that the unrestricted noncommuting case is the target, distinguish it from known small or commuting cases, and keep matrix-order, norm-of-average, and average-of-norm assertions separate.\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"methodology\": {\n        \"baseline\": [\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For positive integer n and finite matrix dimension N, the inputs are P_1,...,P_n in R^(N by N) with P_i^T=P_i and P_i^2=P_i. M is their arithmetic mean and S is the equally weighted sum of their n! label-permuted products. No proof or prerequisite theorem is supplied in the submission.\",\n            \"location\": \"Inline CONFIRMED STATEMENTS[0].statement and SOURCE CONTEXT; CONFIRMED STATEMENTS[0].depends_on is empty.\",\n            \"source\": null\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For arbitrary finite families of real orthogonal projections, (1/n!) sum_sigma P_sigma(n)...P_sigma(1) is at most (1/n) sum_i P_i in Loewner order.\",\n            \"location\": \"Section 4.3, Lemma 3, equation (52), printed page 23 of arXiv v3.\",\n            \"source\": \"On the Efficiency of Random Permutation for ADMM and Coordinate Descent [opened: arXiv:1503.06387]\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For real symmetric positive-semidefinite matrices A and B of the same size, (AB+BA)/2 is at most ((A+B)/2)^2 in Loewner order.\",\n            \"location\": \"Section 3.1, displayed two-matrix identity, printed page 11.6.\",\n            \"source\": \"Toward a Noncommutative Arithmetic-geometric Mean Inequality: Conjectures, Case-studies, and Consequences [opened: https://proceedings.mlr.press/v23/recht12/recht12.pdf]\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"Mutually commuting real symmetric positive-semidefinite matrices admit simultaneous orthogonal diagonalization. The coordinatewise scalar arithmetic-geometric mean comparison then gives product_i A_i \u003c= ((sum_i A_i)/n)^n in Loewner order.\",\n            \"location\": \"Appendix A, Commutative matrices, Theorem 5 and the following simultaneous-diagonalization discussion, printed page 11.15; full-length specialization.\",\n            \"source\": \"Toward a Noncommutative Arithmetic-geometric Mean Inequality: Conjectures, Case-studies, and Consequences [opened: https://proceedings.mlr.press/v23/recht12/recht12.pdf]\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For n\u003e=3 symmetric positive-semidefinite matrices A_i satisfying sum_i A_i\u003c=nI, the average of A_i A_j A_k over pairwise distinct indices satisfies -I\u003c=average\u003c=I. The bound uses the identity matrix, not the cube of the actual arithmetic mean.\",\n            \"location\": \"Section 2, Theorem 2, equation (4).\",\n            \"source\": \"Recht–Ré Noncommutative Arithmetic-Geometric Mean Conjecture is False [opened: arXiv:2006.01510]\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"searched\": [\n          \"Executed targeted web queries included: noncommutative arithmetic geometric mean inequality orthogonal projections Loewner order counterexample; \\\"symmetric idempotent\\\" \\\"mean\\\" \\\"products\\\" inequality; \\\"projection matrices\\\" \\\"arithmetic-geometric\\\".\",\n          \"Executed exact-follow-up queries included: \\\"matrix AM-GM inequality for projection matrices\\\"; \\\"Sun\\\" \\\"Luo\\\" \\\"Ye\\\" \\\"Conjecture 3.1\\\"; \\\"1503.06387\\\" \\\"conjecture\\\" \\\"false\\\"; \\\"projection matrices\\\" \\\"AM-GM\\\" proved; \\\"projection matrices\\\" \\\"AM-GM\\\" disproved.\",\n          \"Executed recency-oriented queries included: \\\"matrix AM-GM\\\" \\\"projection\\\" \\\"conjecture\\\" \\\"2026\\\"; \\\"projection matrices\\\" \\\"arithmetic-geometric\\\" \\\"2025\\\".\",\n          \"The search used web-indexed primary material from arXiv, PMLR proceedings, NeurIPS proceedings, and the INFORMS publisher site. It was not an exhaustive MathSciNet, zbMATH, or citation-network search.\",\n          \"Inspected the exact earlier conjecture, the weak projection inequality, the two-matrix and commuting baselines, the normalized three-factor result, the differently formulated projection counterexample, and the 2025 Appendix E.2 discussion.\",\n          \"Compared all six supplied diagnostic source excerpts as inline text. Their conclusions concern channel fixed states, graph mixing integrality, scalar mean id"},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl_7eb52154-2b84-4164-a8f1-d9df92072e4d","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792098288,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"entities, matrix-cone inclusions, projection valuations, and qutrit effect orthogonality rather than C1.\",\n          \"Listed current-conversation attachments through Files; no uploaded file was available. The root knowledge_queries entry below is a proposed future semantic query, not an already executed search.\"\n        ],\n        \"literature\": \"supported\",\n        \"binding_analysis\": \"Expanding M and S and reversing permutation order identifies the prior conjecture exactly, but a conjecture cannot be invoked as a proved prerequisite. Substitution supplies the two-matrix case; simultaneous diagonalization supplies the commuting restriction. The weak theorem supplies S\u003c=M. Since projection hypotheses give 0\u003c=M\u003c=I and M^n\u003c=M, these inequalities do not compose to yield S\u003c=M^n. Normalized scalar-identity norm bounds do not supply that directional bound either. General-positive-semidefinite counterexamples need not satisfy idempotence, while averaging individual norms changes the conclusion. No supplied diagnostic theorem becomes C1 through the allowed binding operations.\",\n        \"delta\": \"The conditional additional obligation is: for every n\u003e=1, every finite matrix dimension N, every tuple of real symmetric idempotent N-by-N matrices P_i, and every x in R^N, x^T[((sum_i P_i)/n)^n - (1/n!) sum_sigma P_sigma(1)...P_sigma(n)]x\u003e=0. The inspected baseline does not discharge this over the unrestricted noncommuting domain. This is the existing conjectural obligation, not a newly constructed intermediate lemma.\",\n        \"escape\": \"content\",\n        \"witnesses\": [],\n        \"utility\": {\n          \"kind\": \"general_theorem\",\n          \"status\": \"insufficient\",\n          \"rationale\": \"A valid full-domain resolution would have general-theorem utility without needing an artificial downstream consumer. The literature supports the relevance of the question, but the statement-only submission establishes no new mathematical result. The limitation is missing proof or refutation, not missing application.\",\n          \"problem\": \"Does the full permutation average of products of arbitrary real orthogonal projections satisfy S\u003c=M^n in Loewner order for every number of factors and dimension?\",\n          \"progress\": \"The report identifies an exact prior conjecture and separates established restricted or weaker bounds from the unresolved assertion. No new proof, counterexample, algorithm, or certified instance is supplied.\",\n          \"prerequisites\": \"Positive integer number of factors; finite common real matrix dimension; symmetry and idempotence of every factor; equal weighting of all label permutations; no added commutativity, rank, distinctness, or conditioning hypothesis.\",\n          \"discharge\": \"The hypotheses are explicit and match the prior question. The cited established bounds discharge only their stated weaker or restricted conclusions. There is no submitted discharge of the universal target.\"\n        },\n        \"settlement\": null,\n        \"information_gain\": null,\n        \"limits\": [\n          \"This is a statement-only assessment, not a proof verification or attempted solution.\",\n          \"The exact historical identification and baseline comparisons are supported; absence of every later resolution is not certified.\",\n          \"No PDF attachment was available, so manuscript locations refer only to the supplied inline material.\",\n          \"Accessible arXiv and proceedings versions were used. Failed alternative publisher or raw-PDF fetches were not treated as evidence.\",\n          \"The supplied diagnostic publication metadata, hashes, axioms, and verification labels were not independently validated.\",\n          \"No Lean execution, compiled Trureturing registration, quantitative information-gain model, AURIC/FIB measurement, or live proof witness is claimed.\",\n          \"The sources listed under known are known bibliographic comparators, not assertions that C1 is known true.\",\n          \"External claims beyond the opened material remain not_checkable within this report.\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"knowledge_queries\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"C1\",\n      \"definitions\": [\n        \"P_i are real orthogonal projections: P_i^T=P_i and P_i^2=P_i.\",\n        \"M=(1/n) sum_{i=1}^n P_i.\",\n        \"S=(1/n!) sum_{sigma in Sym(n)} P_sigma(1)...P_sigma(n), counting label permutations even when matrices repeat.\",\n        \"M^n-S is positive semidefinite iff x^T(M^n-S)x\u003e=0 for every x in R^N.\"\n      ],\n      \"hypotheses\": [\n        \"n is an integer with n\u003e=1.\",\n        \"N is a finite matrix dimension.\",\n        \"Every P_i is a real N-by-N symmetric idempotent matrix.\"\n      ],\n      \"quantifiers\": \"For every integer n\u003e=1 and every finite matrix dimension N, for every tuple (P_1,...,P_n) satisfying the hypotheses, the conclusion holds. No commutativity, distinctness, rank restriction, or relation between n and N is assumed. N=0 is vacuous if zero-dimensional matrices are allowed.\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"S\u003c=M^n in Loewner order, equivalently M^n-S is positive semidefinite.\",\n      \"search_terms\": [\n        \"orthogonal projection matrix AM-GM conjecture\",\n        \"Sun Luo Ye Conjecture 3.1 equation 37\",\n        \"symmetrized noncommutative product\",\n        \"Loewner order arithmetic-geometric mean\",\n        \"sampling with and without replacement\",\n        \"weak projection AM-GM inequality\",\n        \"noncommutative polynomial positivity with idempotent constraints\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Retrieve a theorem, exact prior formulation, or matching refutation for the projection-specific matrix-order assertion. Distinguish it from general PSD norm inequalities, averages of product norms, and the weaker S\u003c=M bound. This is a proposed search, not evidence of entailment or settlement.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"limits\": [\n    \"Only C1 is assessed; no unseen PDF content is certified.\",\n    \"Open means open on the inspected literature record, subject to the stated bounded-search limitation.\",\n    \"No-match diagnostic retrieval is not evidence of novelty, and nearby counterexamples with changed hypotheses or conclusions do not settle C1.\",\n    \"Conditional proof escape, mathematical relevance, established utility, correctness, and settlement are separate assessments.\",\n    \"No quantitative information gain is inferred from prose or from the number of diagnostic candidates.\",\n    \"All reported sources and comparisons are model-reported, not venue-verified references.\",\n    \"The recommendation is feedback about presentation and evidence, not a publication decision.\"\n  ],\n  \"text\": \"Revise the display to present C1 as the existing projection-matrix AM-GM conjecture with explicit definitions and attribution. Preserve the full universal domain and distinguish the proposed bound from the established weak inequality and from norm-based variants. No proof, refutation, or new result-level utility is established by this submission.\"\n}"},"finish_reason":null}]}

